  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-0{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and $a=0.$ \n\nPlugging $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}{|-\\frac{1}{3}-0|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}{|-\\frac{1}{3}|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[-\\frac{1}{3},0].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|{e}^{x}|={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|={e}^{x}$ is an increasing function on the interval $[-\\frac{1}{3},0],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(0)|={e}^{0}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{1}{24}{|-\\frac{1}{3}|}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24\\cdot {3}^{4}} \\\\  & ≈0.000\\,51,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.",
